{"ts": 0.116302, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;38;5;208m1.2 — keyboard habits\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nFour keys make the shell comfortable. Try each one now.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[38;5;208mTab\u001b[0m       bash finishes a word. Type \u001b[1;38;5;220mecho p\u001b[0m, press \u001b[38;5;208mTab\u001b[0m:\r\n            it becomes \u001b[1;38;5;220mecho poem.txt\u001b[0m. Several matches? The first\r\n            \u001b[38;5;208mTab\u001b[0m fills in the shared part; \u001b[38;5;208mTab\u001b[0m again lists them.\r\n            (Other shells and setups differ.)\r\n  \u001b[38;5;208m↑\u001b[0m         brings back the last command. Enter runs it again.\r\n  \u001b[38;5;208mCtrl+L\u001b[0m    clears the screen; the command \u001b[1;38;5;220mclear\u001b[0m does the same.\r\n            Type \u001b[1;38;5;220mshow\u001b[0m to bring this lesson back afterwards.\r\n  \u001b[38;5;208mCtrl+C\u001b[0m    the escape hatch. If anything feels stuck, press it\r\n            and you get a fresh prompt.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;208m▶ Try each key, then type \u001b[1;38;5;220mnext\u001b[1;38;5;208m.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 891}
