{"ts": 0.272837, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;38;5;208m1.4 — what just happened\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nWhen you type \u001b[1;38;5;220mwhoami\u001b[0m and press Enter:\r\n\r\n  1. The terminal hands your line to \u001b[1;38;5;244mthe shell\u001b[0m.\r\n  2. The shell looks for a program named \u001b[1;38;5;220mwhoami\u001b[0m.\r\n  3. It runs it.\r\n  4. The program prints to \u001b[1;38;5;244mstandard output\u001b[0m — your screen.\r\n  5. The shell shows the prompt again, ready for the next command.\r\n\r\nThe shell is not \"Unix.\" It is a program — a \u001b[1;38;5;244mcommand interpreter\u001b[0m.\r\nThe terminal is a window that lets you talk to the shell. The program\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;220mwhoami\u001b[0m is a third thing — it runs, prints, and exits.\r\n\r\nYou will keep these three apart for the rest of this tutor:\r\n  \u001b[1;38;5;244mterminal\u001b[0m  →  \u001b[1;38;5;244mshell\u001b[0m  →  \u001b[1;38;5;244mprogram\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;38;5;208m▶ Type \u001b[1;38;5;220mnext\u001b[1;38;5;208m to take Stage 1's gate.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 871}
{"ts": 0.273002, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
